The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (hci_dev_test_flag(hdev, HCI_UNREGISTER)) {
    hci_release_dev(hdev);
} else {
    kfree(hdev);
}
Reason:
When HCI device initialization fails before registration, the `HCI_UNREGISTER` flag is never set. Consequently, `bt_host_release()` takes the else branch and calls `kfree(hdev)` directly without first cleaning up the SRCU struct initialized in `hci_alloc_dev()`, causing a percpu memory leak.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
} else {
    cleanup_srcu_struct(&hdev->srcu);
    kfree(hdev);
}
Reason:
Adding `cleanup_srcu_struct(&hdev->srcu)` before the `kfree(hdev)` ensures that the percpu SRCU memory is properly released even when the device was never registered, thereby fixing the memory leak.

